---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Resources — Textbooks and Study Tools | devsyllabus"
  description="Curated resources for MBBS study: the standard textbooks by subject, free authoritative references, and how to use them alongside our notes."
  path="/resources/"
>
  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ label: 'Resources' }]} />

  <div class="doc">
  <h1>Resources</h1>
  <p>
    The sources our notes cite, plus tools worth knowing. These are
    recommendations only — see our
    <a href="/sources/">source citation policy</a> and
    <a href="/disclaimer/">disclaimer</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Standard textbooks by subject</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <em>Gray's Anatomy for Students</em> (Drake, Vogl, and Mitchell) and
      <em>Snell's Clinical Anatomy by Regions</em> — the region-wise and
      clinical approaches respectively; most first-year courses expect one
      of these alongside an atlas such as <em>Netter's</em>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <em>Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology</em> — the
      standard for physiology; <em>Ganong's Review of Medical
      Physiology</em> is the condensed alternative many students revise
      from.
    </li>
    <li>
      <em>Harper's Illustrated Biochemistry</em> — the standard
      biochemistry text; <em>Lippincott Illustrated Reviews:
      Biochemistry</em> is the common outline-format companion.
    </li>
    <li>
      <em>Robbins &amp; Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease</em> — the
      reference for general and systemic pathology.
    </li>
    <li>
      <em>Katzung's Basic &amp; Clinical Pharmacology</em> — prototypes
      first, mechanisms clearly laid out; <em>Lippincott Illustrated
      Reviews: Pharmacology</em> for quick revision.
    </li>
    <li>
      <em>Jawetz, Melnick &amp; Adelberg's Medical Microbiology</em> —
      organism-by-organism coverage matched to how professional exams
      test it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <em>Knight's Forensic Pathology</em> and <em>Parikh's Textbook of
      Medical Jurisprudence</em> — the standard forensic medicine pairing
      in our region.
    </li>
    <li>
      <em>Park's Textbook of Preventive and Social Medicine</em> — the
      reference for community medicine, especially the national programme
      and biostatistics material.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Free authoritative references</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <a href="https://www.who.int/health-topics" rel="noopener">WHO health topics</a>
      — fact sheets and guidance useful for community medicine and
      communicable-disease notes.
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/" rel="noopener">PubMed</a>
      — the primary-literature index, for checking claims that go beyond
      textbook teaching.
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href="https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/" rel="noopener">NCBI Bookshelf</a>
      — free full-text access to several standard medical texts.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How to use these</h2>
  <p>
    Study the devsyllabus notes first, then verify against the textbook
    when a detail matters. That habit is what our
    <a href="/blog/active-recall-spaced-repetition/">study technique article</a>
    recommends for exam-prep anyway: retrieval first, reference second.
  </p>
  </div>
</Layout>
